// C primitive: `for x in s` iterates any value whose type declares one
// function applying @iteration. It takes a position and returns the element
// there, or absence past the end.
//
// Law, checked in C because macro bodies cannot inspect a function's types
// yet: a type declares exactly one @iteration function, and it takes one Int
// parameter and returns an optional, the enum applying @optional.
//
// `for pattern in s where guard { body }` copies s when the loop begins and
// calls the function at positions 0, 1, 2, …; on absence the loop ends.
// Otherwise the element is bound to pattern (a let), the guard applies, the
// body runs, and the position advances by one, also on `continue`. C writes
// the loop and finds the function by this role; it never names the function,
// its parameter, or the type.
@builtin
macro iteration(): Function
